## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a novel class of single-site solid catalysts in the past few years.1-16 MOFs are typically endowed with catalytic activity either through covalent incorporation of catalytically active species into the organic ligands or the inorganic nodes17-19 or through entrapment of metal nanoparticles,20-21 enzymes22, or inorganic clusters in the channels.23-24 One class of MOF catalysts distinguishes itself from other catalysts by using the inorganic nodes as single-site catalytic centers.  The metal centers in these MOFs feature unique electronic properties and steric environments that are not accessible to conventional homogeneous or heterogeneous catalytic species.25-
27  In this context, early demonstrations of MOF catalysis took advantage of Lewis acidity of the metal-connecting points, 28-29 but these MOFs are relatively unstable and do not possess high catalytic activities. The advent of highly stable MOFs based on early metals of ${\bf d}^{0}$ electronic configurations30-31 brought about porous solid catalysts with enhanced Lewis acidity for a number of catalytic reactions such as isomerization, 32-33 hydrolysis,34 and dehydration35 among others.36-39
Despite recent progress,40-46 existing MOFs exhibit significantly lower Lewis acidity than homogeneous systems such as $\mathrm{Sc(OTf)}_{3}$ . In contrast to homogeneous systems, where Lewis acidity at the active sites can be precisely measured, no techniques are currently available to quantitatively assess Lewis acidity of MOF catalysts due, in part, to the insolubility and structural complexity of MOFs. Although notable efforts have been made to use reaction kinetics to probe Lewis acidity, the reaction rates are convoluted with intrinsic Lewis acidity, diffusion rates of substrates/products through the MOF channels and in solution, and partition of substrates/products between MOF channels.32, 47-48 The objectives of this work are to develop quantitative methods to determine the Lewis acidity of MOFs and to discover effective strategies to enhance their Lewis acidity. Significant enhancement of Lewis acidity of MOFs can afford porous solid catalysts with much larger pores and channels than zeolites for fine chemical synthesis.
We report here the development of two general methods for determining Lewis acidity of MOFs. The first method probes Lewis acidity through the coordination of in situ generated superoxide which displays diagnostic electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) features. The second method probes the Lewis acidity through the binding of a fluorescent dye with strongly coordinative electron lone pairs.49 We prepared a series of UiO MOFs featuring defect $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ inorganic nodes with missing linkers and unsaturated coordination around $Z\mathrm{r}$ active sites. 50-55 Application of EPR and fluorescence methods to a variety of Lewis acidic MOFs, especially the MOFs with defect $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes, has unveiled a strong correlation between dicarboxylate electron density and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ Lewis acidity. Based on this insight, we have significantly enhanced the Lewis acidity of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs using perfluorinated bridging ligands to afford porous solid catalysts for challenging transformations typically effected by strongly Lewis acidic homogeneous catalysts (Figure 1).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis and Characterization of Perfluorinated MOFs. We have synthesized two perfluorinated UiO-MOFs, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC, by replacing 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC) in $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ - BDC (UiO-66) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate (BPDC) ligands in $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BPDC (UiO-67) with 2,3,5,6-tetrafluoro-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate (fBDC) and $2,2^{\prime},3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}$ -octafluoro$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate (fBPDC) ligands, respectively (Figure 2). $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was synthesized in $86\%$ yield by heating a mixture of $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}f B D C}$ in acidic tetrahydrofuran (THF) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) studies indicated that $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC is isostructural to UiO-66 (Figure S6, SI). The porosity of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, which displayed a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of 1021 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . DFT fitting of the ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC gave pore sizes of ${\sim}6$ and ${\sim}8\mathrm{\AA}$ that are attributable to the tetrahedral and octahedral cages of the UiO structure, respectively (Figure S7, SI), in addition to a larger pore at ${\sim}12\mathrm{~\AA~}$ due to the missing fBDC ligands. The surface area of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC is lower than that of UiO-66 due to the increased formula weight in the perfluorinated MOF (1875 Da for $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC vs 1664 Da for UiO-66). Unlike previously reported perfluorinated MOFs,56-60 $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC features high thermal stability with a decomposition temperature of $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as measured by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure S8, SI). A combination of $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR and ICP-MS techniques showed the number of missing linkers to be 1.33 per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node (Figure S5, SI). Based on this information, the formula of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC is calculated to be $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}{-}O)_{4}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{4}(f B D C)_{4.67}[(O H)(O H_{2})]_{2.66}}$ . The defect density of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC is slightly larger than that of defect UiO-66, which was reported to be 0.7 missing linkers per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node, presumably due to the relatively weaker coordination strength of the fBDC ligand to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node.61 The structure of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was unaltered after desolvation and catalysis based on the retention of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC PXRD patterns.
$\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC, a UiO-67 analogue of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC with larger channels, was prepared similarly. The $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}f B P D C}$ linker was synthesized by $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ -mediated oxidative aryl-aryl coupling of the methyl tetrafluorobenzoate organozinc reagent followed by ester hydrolysis (Figure S1, SI). $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC was synthesized in $90\%$ yield by heating a mixture of $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}f B P D C}$ in acidic THF at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC matched well with the simulated pattern for UiO-67, indicating their isostructural nature (Figure S11, SI). Compared to $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC has a larger BET surface area of $1148~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ while maintaining high thermal stability with a decomposition temperature of $310^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as measured by TGA (Figure S12-13, SI). $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC displayed two types of pores at ${\sim}12$ and ${\sim}15\mathrm{\AA},$ corresponding to the tetrahedral and octahedral cages, respectively, and a larger pore at ${\sim}22\mathrm{~\AA~}$ due to the missing fBPDC ligands. The number of missing linkers per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node was determined by $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR and ICP-MS to be 1.73 per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node, giving the formula $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O)_{4}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{4}(f B P D C)_{4.27}[(O H)(O H_{2})]_{3.46}}$ for $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC (Figure S10, SI).
## Synthesis and Characterization of Nitrated MOFs
$\mathrm{Zr_{6}–B D C–N O_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{BPDC-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}$ were synthesized to compare their Lewis acidity with those of perfluorinated MOFs. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ ${\bf B D C-N O}_{2}$ was prepared from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-NO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ in acidified DMF.  PXRD studies indicated its isostructural nature with UiO-66. $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ afforded a BET surface area of $\mathrm{{\dot{960}}~m^{2}/g}$ and pore diameters of ${\sim}6$ , ${\sim}8$ , and ${\sim}12\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure S18, SI). The similar porosity of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}B D C{\cdot}N O_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC makes them a good pair to study the impact of Lewis acidity on catalytic performance without the complication of varying diffusion kinetics through MOF channels. The number of missing linkers was determined by $^{1}\mathrm{H\NMR}$ and ICP-MS to be 0.94 per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node, thus giving $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}–\mathbf{N}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ the formula $\mathrm{{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}-}$ $\mathrm{O)}_{4}(\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC}{\mathrm{-NO}}_{2})_{5.06}[(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{OH}_{2})]_{1.88}$ (Figure S16, SI). The defect density of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}{\mathbf{C}}–\mathbf{N}{\mathbf{O}}_{2}$ is essentially the same as that of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC.
$\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{BPDC-}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{2})_{2}$ with larger channels was synthesized in $85\%$ yield by heating a mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ in acidified THF at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}$ ligand was synthesized in high yield using the Pd-catalyzed Suzuki-Miyaura cross-coupling reaction followed by ester hydrolysis (Figure S4, SI). $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BPDC- $(\mathsf{N O}_{2})_{2}$ is isostructural to UiO-67 based on the similarity of their PXRD patterns (Figure S21, SI). The porosity of The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{BPDC-}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{2})_{2}$ was confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, affording a BET surface area of $1205~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and pore diameters of ${\sim}12$ , ${\sim}15$ , and ${\sim}22\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure S22, SI).
## Probing Lewis acidity by superoxide EPR spectra
The EPR spectra of superoxide ions bound to a variety of UiO MOFs were measured on a Bruker Elexsys $500\mathrm{~X~}$ -band EPR spectrometer at $100~\mathrm{K}$ . A clear trend was observed for the measured $\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ values. UiO MOFs grown from more electron-deficient dicarboxylates, namely those with electron-withdrawing groups, showed smaller $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ values for the anisotropic EPR spectra, indicating larger ∆E values. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BDC produced a $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ value of 2.0344, which corresponds to a $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.8735{\mathrm{~eV}}$ . This $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ is the smallest value in the UiO-66 MOF series in this study. The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{BDC-NO}_{2}$ MOF, which was previously reported to catalyze $(+)$ -citronellal isomerization,32 showed a smaller $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ value of 2.0330, which corresponds to a larger $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.9114\ \mathrm{eV}$ . Impressively, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC exhibited a $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ of 2.0324 and a $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.9304{\mathrm{~eV}}$ , making it the most Lewis acidic MOF in the UiO-66 series.
To validate the trend observed for the UiO-66 series, two new MOFs of UiO-67 topology were prepared. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BPDC showed a $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ of 2.0343 and a $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.8743\ \mathrm{eV}$ , similar to that of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BDC. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{BPDC-}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{2})_{2}$ gave a $\bf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ of 2.0334 and a $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.9014{\mathrm{~eV}}$ . The perfluorinated MOF, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC, is much more Lewis acidic with a $\bf{g}_{\mathrm{zz}}$ of 2.0326 and a $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ of $0.9246{\mathrm{~eV}}$ . These experiments prove that perfluorination is an effective way to enhance Lewis acidity of UiO-MOFs with defect $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes.
## Density Functional Theory (DFT) Calculations of Superoxide Binding on Zr Active Sites
DFT calculations were also used to elucidate the impact of defect density on MOF Lewis acidity. For $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC, two additional models for superoxide-bound MOF nodes, one with two missing carboxylates $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC-2def- ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{\bullet\cdot}$ ) and another with three missing carboxylates $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC-2def- ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{\bullet\bullet}$ ), were optimized and analyzed for SOMO-HOMO gaps (Section 5.3, SI). The superoxide moieties in the doubly or triply defected models adopt identical coordination modes as the singly defected model and produced identical SOMO-HOMO gaps of $2.84~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S39-42, SI). The inclusion of more defects, unlike the exchange of carboxylates, does not alter the primary coordination environment around Lewis acidic $Z\mathrm{r}$ centers.
## Probing Lewis acidity by NMA fluorescence
The coordination mode and electronic structure of NMA bound to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node was also studied by DFT. The optimized structure showed that the carbonyl group (instead of the aryl or nitrogen) is coordinated to the vacant site of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node, adopting a side-on coordination mode. Molecular orbital calculations of the NMA fluorescent dye revealed the electronic structure of the HOMO orbital, which is proposed to be responsible for the NMA coordination to $Z\mathrm{r}$ (Figure S43, SI).
To exclude the possibility that the Brønsted acidic $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ (pKa $\sim3.52)^{66}$ might have caused the shift in the NMA emission, a series of Brønsted acids (trifluoroacetic acid, benzoic acid, acetic acid, and 4-aminobenzoic acid) covering the pKa range of $-0.25\sim$ 4.86 were mixed with NMA in MeCN to measure fluorescence. None of the four acids caused any shift in the NMA emission spectra, indicating Brønsted acids are not responsible for the shifts in NMA fluorescence in the UiO series (Figure S27, SI). For weak Lewis acids that have ∆E values less than $0.5\mathrm{eV}$ , NMA cannot coordinate to the metal centers effectively thus leading to almost no shift in emission wavelength.49 Two common Lewis acidic MOFs, MOF-5 and HKUST-1,67-68 displayed no shift in the NMA fluorescence, indicating the $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ node in MOF-5 and the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ node in HKUST-1 have ∆E values less than $0.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S29, SI).
## Perfluorinated MOF-Catalyzed Diels-Alder Reactions
The synthesis of perfluorinated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs and the characterization of their prominent Lewis acidity inspired the investigation of their catalytic activities. We first tested perfluorinated $Z\mathrm{r}$ MOFs for Diels-Alder reactions.
The Diels-Alder reaction is of great synthetic value to the organic chemistry community and has been widely applied to the synthesis of six-member ring structures with high regioselectivity and stereoselectivity.69-70 However, the reaction generally requires high temperatures and long reaction times when performed without the addition of an acid catalyst. Unlike classical Lewis acidic metal complexes, MOF catalysts benefit from having uniform active sites which can lead to better reaction selectivity. The solid nature of MOFs offers additional advantages, including easy separation from reaction mixtures and catalyst reusability. For the cyclization of 1,4-benzoquinone with 2,3-dimethylbuta-1,3-diene, only $4\%$ yield of product was observed in the absence of catalyst at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ (entry 1, Table 2). In the presence of $\mathrm{\angler_{6}-B D C}$ , which has been shown to be active for ethanol dehydration at 250 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ,35 the product yield increased to $14\%$ under the same reaction conditions (entry 2, Table 2). $\mathrm{Zr_{6}}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}C–\mathbf{N}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , which is reported to be active for $\left(+\right)$ -citronellal isomerization at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , increased the reaction yield to $25\%$ at the same reaction condition (entry 3, Table 2). Under the same reaction conditions, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC afforded the cycloaddition product in $78\%$ yield. Elongation of the reaction time from $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to $8\mathrm{{h}}$ further increased the yield to $98\%$ . Considering $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC has a higher density of defect sites, additional control experiments were performed by increasing the catalyst loading of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}C$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}}–\mathbf{BDC}–\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ to the same loading of defect sites as $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC. However, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC still afforded the product at much higher yield than $\mathrm{Zr}_{6^{-}}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}–\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in these experiments (Table S5, SI). The reaction is proposed to be initiated by the coordination of the lone pair electrons of the quinones to the $Z\mathrm{r}$ centers.  This coordination is expected to lower the energy of the LUMO orbital to enhance the overlap with the HOMO orbital of the dienes. The coordination of quinones to $Z\mathrm{r}$ is proposed to be analogous to the coordination of NMA to $\mathrm{Zr}$ , since they both contain electron-rich carbonyl groups.
The MOF-catalyzed Diels-Alder reaction displayed a broad substrate scope (entry 5-10, Table 2). Cyclohexa-1,3-diene, a less reactive diene than cyclopentadiene or 2,3-dimethylbuta-1,3- diene, was cyclized with 1,4-benzoquinone in $94\%$ yield in 12h in the presence of $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC.71 Quinone substrates containing electron-donating groups, such as the 2-methyl-1,4- benzoquinone, are generally less reactive in Diels-Alder reactions due to their much higher LUMO energy. The presence of a methyl group also greatly increases the steric hindrance of the quinone substrate. Impressively, in the presence of $5\mathrm{\mol}\%$ $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ 一 fBDC, 2-methyl-1,4-benzoquinone efficiently reacted with cyclohexa-1,3-diene and 2,3-dimethylbuta-1,3-diene at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford cycloaddition products in $74\%$ and $84\%$ yields, respectively. Moreover, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was reused for at least three runs without significant loss of catalytic activity for the cyclization of 2,3- dimethylbuta-1,3-diene with 2-chloro-1,4-benzoquinone (Figure S44, SI).
## Perfluorinated MOF-Catalyzed Arene C-H Iodination Reactions
Aryl iodides are very useful building blocks in organic synthesis. The C-I bonds of arenes readily undergo cross-coupling reactions and nucleophilic substitutions.72-73 There are multiple synthetic methods to access these compounds, including the Sandmeyer reaction, halide exchange reactions, and electrophilic substitutions. The most straightforward method is the direct C-H iodination using iodine or N-iodosuccinimide (NIS).74-75 Many acid catalysts have been developed to perform such reactions, notably the $\mathrm{BF}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ system reported by Olah and co-workers which could iodinate very challenging electron-deficient arenes.76 For electron-rich arenes, however, many catalysts suffer from poor regioselectvity, leading to the formation of multi-iodinated products.77 $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was tested for the iodination of anisole at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using MeCN as the solvent. These conditions afforded exclusively the mono-idodination product in $96\%$ yield after $30\mathrm{h}$ . Other arene substrates, such as 2-methoxynaphthalene, aniline, and $\mathbf{N}.$ - methylaniline, were also iodinated in high yields with excellent regioselectivity (entry 5-8, Table 3).
The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was compared to other MOFs to show the importance of ligand perfluorination. At identical reaction conditions and catalyst loading, $\mathrm{\calZr}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ - ${\bf B D C-N O}_{2}$ only iodinated 2-methoxynaphthalene in $16\%$ and $28\%$ yield, respectively, while $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC gave $85\%$ yield of the 3- iodinated product. Even when the catalyst loadings of $Z_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{I}}_{6}-B D C}}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}}–\mathbf{BDC}–\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ were increased to the same loading of defect sites as $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC, the yields of product were still much lower than that of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC (Table S6, SI). Other Lewis acidic MOF catalysts, e.g., MOF-5 and HKUST-1, also displayed much lower catalytic performance than $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC (Table S8). Impressively, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC could be recycled at least 6 times without a significant drop in catalytic activity, illustrating the robustness of the solid Lewis acid catalyst (Figure S47, SI).
## CONCLUSION
We developed two general methods for quantifying the Lewis acidity of MOFs by measuring the changes in superoxide EPR signals and shifts of NMA fluorescence peaks upon their coordination to MOFs. We prepared a series of UiO-MOFs featuring defect $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes with missing linkers and unsaturated coordination around $Z\mathrm{r}$ active sites. The application of the EPR and fluorescence methods to a variety of MOFs unveiled a strong correlation between dicarboxylate electron density and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ Lewis acidity, leading to the discovery of significantly enhanced Lewis acidity within $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs containing perfluorinated bridging ligands.  The new perfluorinated MOFs, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBPDC, are significantly more Lewis acidic than nonsubstituted UiO-66 and UiO-67 as well as the nitrated MOFs $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}.$ - ${\bf B D C-N O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -BPDC- $\cdot(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}$ . $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC was shown to be a highly active Lewis acid catalyst for Diels-Alder and arene C-H iodination reactions. This work, thus, establishes the important role of ligand perfluorination in enhancing MOF Lewis acidity and the potential of designing new, highly Lewis acidic MOFs as single-site porous solid catalysts for fine chemical synthesis.
